INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON THE TRANSFER STATUTE
In 1948, Congress enacted a statute authorizing the transfer of cases between federal district courts “[f]or the convenience of the parties and witnesses [and] the interest of justice.” These statutes govern requests by parties to move an action filed in one federal district or division to a different federal district or division. (As we’ve noted elsewhere, a division is the part of a federal district served by a single courthouse. Thus, a district with two courthouses will be divided into two divisions. A district with only one courthouse does not have divisions.) Note that these statutes give no authority to federal courts to transfer cases to courts outside the federal system. To move a case from federal court to a state court or to a foreign court still requires the federal court to dismiss the action without prejudice.
